@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- California interpretation provisions: how statutes and codes operate, amend, repeal and conflict.
--
-- Sources: Gov. Code §§ 2-5, 9, 9604-9611; Civ. Code §§ 3, 5, 6, 20, 22.2, 23-23.6; Code Civ. Proc. §§ 3, 5-9, 18.
-- The quotation lines are copied by script from the converter-made plain-text rendering of the sections (not the official text)
-- and checked against it by srcrefs.py.
--
-- Each rule is a function of facts the caller supplies as inputs (a date, a yes-or-no fact, a kind).  A case the text does not
-- answer is a named REFUSE, never a default.  Target: unstable (REFUSE).  Imports: prelude and daydate.

§ `Repeal and revival`

-- Gov. Code § 9606: a statute may be repealed at any time, except when vested rights would be impaired.
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:25 | 9606. Any statute may be repealed at any time, except when vested rights would be impaired. Persons acting under any statute act in contemplation of this power of repeal.
@ref Gov. Code § 9606 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:25)
GIVEN `vested rights would be impaired` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a statute may be repealed` `vested rights would be impaired` MEANS
    NOT `vested rights would be impaired`

-- § 9607(a): "no statute or part of a statute, repealed by another statute, is revived by the repeal of the repealing statute without
-- express words reviving such repealed statute or part of a statute".  The exception in (b) is the next function.
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:27 | 9607. (a) Except as provided in subdivision (b), no statute or part of a statute, repealed by another statute, is revived by the repeal of the repealing statute without express words reviving such repealed statute or part of a statute.
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:28 | (b) If a later enacted statute that deletes or extends the date of termination or repeal of a previously enacted law is chaptered before such date of termination or repeal, the terminated or repealed law is revived when the later enacted statute becomes operative.
@ref Gov. Code § 9607(a) (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:27-28)
GIVEN `the repealing statute is itself repealed` IS A BOOLEAN
      `express words revive the repealed statute` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a repealed statute is revived by the repeal of the statute that repealed it` `the repealing statute is itself repealed` `express words revive the repealed statute` MEANS
    `the repealing statute is itself repealed` AND `express words revive the repealed statute`

-- § 9607(b): "If a later enacted statute that deletes or extends the date of termination or repeal of a previously enacted law is
-- chaptered before such date of termination or repeal, the terminated or repealed law is revived when the later enacted statute
-- becomes operative."  The text does not say whether the date of termination is the last day the law operates or the first day it
-- does not (fork F-9607b in NOTES); this function compares the two dates as they are stated, "before" meaning earlier than.
@ref Gov. Code § 9607(b) (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:27-28)
GIVEN chaptered IS A DATE
      `the stated date of termination or repeal` IS A DATE
      `the later statute becomes operative on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`the day a terminated or repealed law is revived by a later statute chaptered on` chaptered `the stated date of termination or repeal` `the later statute becomes operative on` MEANS
    IF chaptered LESS THAN `the stated date of termination or repeal`
    THEN JUST `the later statute becomes operative on`
    ELSE NOTHING

-- § 9608: the termination or suspension of a law creating a criminal offense "does not constitute a bar to the indictment or
-- information and punishment of an act already committed in violation of the law so terminated or suspended, unless the intention to
-- bar ... is expressly declared by an applicable provision of law."
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:30 | 9608. The termination or suspension (by whatsoever means effected) of any law creating a criminal offense does not constitute a bar to the indictment or information and punishment of an act already committed in violation of the law so terminated or suspended, unless the intention to bar such indictment or information and punishment is expressly declared by an applicable provision of law.
@ref Gov. Code § 9608 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:30)
GIVEN `the act was committed in violation of the law before it was terminated or suspended` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a provision of law expressly declares the intention to bar` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the termination or suspension of a criminal law bars indictment, information and punishment` `the act was committed in violation of the law before it was terminated or suspended` `a provision of law expressly declares the intention to bar` MEANS
    IF `the act was committed in violation of the law before it was terminated or suspended`
    THEN `a provision of law expressly declares the intention to bar`
    ELSE REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9608 speaks of an act already committed in violation of the law terminated or suspended; it does not say what follows for an act that was not committed in violation of the law before it ended"

-- § 9609: "A statute amending a section of a repealed statute is void."
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:32 | 9609. A statute amending a section of a repealed statute is void.
@ref Gov. Code § 9609 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:32)
GIVEN `the statute amended was repealed when the amending statute was enacted` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 9609 makes a statute amending a section of a repealed statute void` `the statute amended was repealed when the amending statute was enacted` MEANS
    `the statute amended was repealed when the amending statute was enacted`

-- § 9610: fixing a salary by statute "is not intended to and does not constitute an appropriation"; "The salary shall be paid only in
-- the event that moneys are made available therefor by another provision of law."
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:34 | 9610. The fixing or authorizing the fixing of the salary of a State officer or employee by statute is not intended to and does not constitute an appropriation of money for the payment of the salary. The salary shall be paid only in the event that moneys are made available therefor by another provision of law.
@ref Gov. Code § 9610 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:34)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a statute fixing the salary of a State officer or employee is an appropriation of money for it` MEANS FALSE

@ref Gov. Code § 9610 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:34)
GIVEN `another provision of law makes moneys available for the salary` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the salary fixed by statute shall be paid` `another provision of law makes moneys available for the salary` MEANS
    `another provision of law makes moneys available for the salary`

-- § 9611: a law "temporarily suspended, or ... expressly or impliedly modified or repealed by a provision which is declared to be
-- effective for only a limited period" is not deemed repealed; "upon the expiration of the time of the temporary suspension or the
-- effectiveness of the inconsistent provision, the original provision shall have the same force and effect as if the temporary
-- provision had not been enacted."  The input is the last day on which the temporary provision is effective; the original has its
-- full force from the day after (fork F-9611 in NOTES: the text says "upon the expiration", not on which day).
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:36 | 9611. Notwithstanding any other provision of this chapter, whenever a provision of law is temporarily suspended, or is expressly or impliedly modified or repealed by a provision which is declared to be effective for only a limited period, the original provisions are not to be deemed repealed, but upon the expiration of the time of the temporary suspension or the effectiveness of the inconsistent provision, the original provision shall have the same force and effect as if the temporary provision had not been enacted.
@ref Gov. Code § 9611 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:36)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a law temporarily suspended or modified is deemed repealed` MEANS FALSE

@ref Gov. Code § 9611 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:36)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
      `the last day on which the temporary provision is effective` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the original provision has its full force again on the day` d `after a temporary provision effective through` `the last day on which the temporary provision is effective` MEANS
    d GREATER THAN `the last day on which the temporary provision is effective`

§ `Amendment`

DECLARE `Provision after an amendment` IS ONE OF
    `unaltered by the amendment`
    `new in the amendment`
    `omitted by the amendment`

-- § 9605(a)(1): "The unaltered provisions are to be considered as having been the law from the time when those provisions were enacted.
-- The new provisions are to be considered as having been enacted at the time of the amendment.  The omitted provisions are to be
-- considered as having been repealed at the time of the amendment."
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:17 | 9605. (a) (1) When a section or part of a statute is amended, it is not to be considered as having been repealed and reenacted in the amended form. The unaltered provisions are to be considered as having been the law from the time when those provisions were enacted. The new provisions are to be considered as having been enacted at the time of the amendment. The omitted provisions are to be considered as having been repealed at the time of the amendment.
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:18 | (2) When the same section or part of a statute is amended by two or more acts enacted at the same session, any portion of provisions from an earlier one of those successive acts that are omitted by a subsequent act
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:19 | shall be deemed to have been omitted deliberately and any provisions omitted by an earlier act that are restored by a subsequent act shall be deemed to have been restored deliberately.
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:20 | (b) When the same section or part of a statute is amended by two or more statutes enacted at the same session:
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:21 | (1) In the absence of any express provision to the contrary in the statute that is enacted last, it shall be conclusively presumed that the statute that is enacted last is intended to prevail over statutes that are enacted earlier at the same session.
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:22 | (2) In the absence of any express provision to the contrary in the statute with a higher chapter number, it shall be presumed that the statute with a higher chapter number is intended
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:23 | by the Legislature to prevail over a statute that is enacted at the same session with a lower chapter number. For the purposes of this paragraph, every statute enacted in the even-numbered year of a two-year regular session of the Legislature is deemed to bear a higher chapter number than any statute enacted in the odd-numbered year of that session.
@ref Gov. Code § 9605(a)(1) (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:17-23)
GIVEN kind IS A `Provision after an amendment`
      `the day the provision was first enacted` IS A DATE
      `the day of the amendment` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day a provision of an amended section is considered to have been enacted` kind `the day the provision was first enacted` `the day of the amendment` MEANS
    CONSIDER kind
    WHEN `unaltered by the amendment` THEN `the day the provision was first enacted`
    WHEN `new in the amendment` THEN `the day of the amendment`
    WHEN `omitted by the amendment` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9605(a)(1) treats an omitted provision as repealed at the time of the amendment, not as enacted then"

@ref Gov. Code § 9605(a)(1) (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:17-23)
GIVEN kind IS A `Provision after an amendment`
      `the day of the amendment` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`the day a provision of an amended section is considered to have been repealed` kind `the day of the amendment` MEANS
    CONSIDER kind
    WHEN `omitted by the amendment` THEN `the day of the amendment`
    WHEN `unaltered by the amendment` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9605(a)(1) says an amended section is not considered repealed and reenacted, and treats only the omitted provisions as repealed"
    WHEN `new in the amendment` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9605(a)(1) treats the new provisions as enacted at the time of the amendment, not as repealed"

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an amended section is considered repealed and reenacted in the amended form` MEANS FALSE

-- § 9605(a)(2): the same section amended by two or more acts at one session.  A portion that an earlier act has and a later act omits is
-- "deemed to have been omitted deliberately"; a portion an earlier act omits and a later act restores is "deemed to have been restored
-- deliberately".  So the last act in the order of enactment decides whether the portion stands.  The list says, for each act in the order
-- enacted, whether its text of the section contains the portion.
@ref Gov. Code § 9605(a)(2) (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:17-23)
GIVEN `whether each act in order of enactment contains the portion` IS A LIST OF BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a portion stands after successive acts of one session amend the same section` `whether each act in order of enactment contains the portion` MEANS
    CONSIDER reverse `whether each act in order of enactment contains the portion`
    WHEN EMPTY THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9605(a)(2) speaks of a section amended by two or more acts; no act was given"
    WHEN x FOLLOWED BY rest THEN x

DECLARE `Amending statute` HAS
    `order of enactment`                                                              IS A NUMBER
    `chapter number`                                                                  IS A NUMBER
    `enacted in the even-numbered year of a two-year regular session`                 IS A BOOLEAN
    `says expressly that it is not to prevail`                                        IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `Which of two statutes` IS ONE OF
    `the first statute`
    `the second statute`

-- The chapter number as § 9605(b)(2) deems it: "every statute enacted in the even-numbered year of a two-year regular session of the
-- Legislature is deemed to bear a higher chapter number than any statute enacted in the odd-numbered year of that session."
GIVEN s IS AN `Amending statute`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the chapter rank of` s MEANS
    IF s's `enacted in the even-numbered year of a two-year regular session` THEN 1000000 PLUS s's `chapter number` ELSE s's `chapter number`

-- § 9605(b): two statutes amending the same section at the same session.  (1) "In the absence of any express provision to the contrary in
-- the statute that is enacted last, it shall be conclusively presumed that the statute that is enacted last is intended to prevail".
-- (2) "In the absence of any express provision to the contrary in the statute with a higher chapter number, it shall be presumed that the
-- statute with a higher chapter number is intended by the Legislature to prevail".  The text does not rank (1) against (2) when they point
-- at different statutes (fork F-9605b in NOTES), and says nothing when neither presumption is open.
-- Two statutes with the same order of enactment have no statute "enacted last", so (1) is not open and (2) alone decides: the higher
-- chapter rank prevails unless that statute expressly provides otherwise.  (Changed after the independent test pass: the first version
-- refused here, before reaching (2).)  That (1) says "conclusively presumed" and (2) only "presumed" is the reading F-9605b names.
@ref Gov. Code § 9605(b) (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:17-23)
GIVEN first IS AN `Amending statute`
      second IS AN `Amending statute`
GIVETH A `Which of two statutes`
`the statute that prevails when two statutes of one session amend the same section` first second MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF first's `order of enactment` EQUALS second's `order of enactment`
            THEN IF chapterIsOpen
                 THEN IF higherIsFirst THEN `the first statute` ELSE `the second statute`
                 ELSE REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9605(b)(2) gives no presumption between two statutes of the same order of enactment when their chapter ranks are equal or the statute with the higher chapter number expressly provides that it is not to prevail, and (b)(1) has no statute enacted last"
        IF lastIsOpen AND chapterIsOpen AND NOT lastIsFirst EQUALS higherIsFirst
            THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9605(b)(1) and (b)(2) point at different statutes, and the text does not say which presumption governs"
        IF lastIsOpen THEN IF lastIsFirst THEN `the first statute` ELSE `the second statute`
        IF chapterIsOpen THEN IF higherIsFirst THEN `the first statute` ELSE `the second statute`
        OTHERWISE REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9605(b) gives no presumption when the statute enacted last, and the statute with the higher chapter number, each expressly provide that they are not to prevail, or when the chapter ranks are equal"
    WHERE
        lastIsFirst MEANS first's `order of enactment` GREATER THAN second's `order of enactment`
        lastIsOpen MEANS NOT (IF lastIsFirst THEN first's `says expressly that it is not to prevail` ELSE second's `says expressly that it is not to prevail`)
        higherIsFirst MEANS `the chapter rank of` first GREATER THAN `the chapter rank of` second
        chapterIsOpen MEANS (NOT `the chapter rank of` first EQUALS `the chapter rank of` second)
            AND NOT (IF higherIsFirst THEN first's `says expressly that it is not to prevail` ELSE second's `says expressly that it is not to prevail`)

§ `References and continuations`

DECLARE `Kind of code` IS ONE OF
    `the Government Code`
    `the Civil Code`
    `the Code of Civil Procedure`

-- Gov. Code § 2, Civ. Code § 5, Code Civ. Proc. § 5: provisions "substantially the same as existing" law "shall be construed as
-- restatements and continuations, and not as new enactments."  Gov. Code § 2 speaks of "existing statutory provisions relating to the same
-- subject matter", Civ. Code § 5 of "existing statutes or the common law", Code Civ. Proc. § 5 of "existing statutes" only.
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:10 | 2. The provisions of this code in so far as they are substantially the same as existing statutory provisions relating to the same subject matter shall be construed as restatements and continuations, and not as new enactments.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:20 | 5. The provisions of this Code, so far as they are substantially the same as existing statutes or the common law, must be construed as continuations thereof, and not as new enactments.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:20 | 5. The provisions of this Code, so far as they are substantially the same as existing statutes, must be construed as continuations thereof, and not as new enactments.
@ref Gov. Code § 2, Civ. Code § 5, Code Civ. Proc. § 5 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:10, src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:20, src:CCP-ss-1-34:20)
GIVEN code IS A `Kind of code`
      `substantially the same as an existing statutory provision on the same subject` IS A BOOLEAN
      `substantially the same as the common law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the section construes a provision of` code `as a restatement and continuation, given that it is substantially the same as an existing statute:` `substantially the same as an existing statutory provision on the same subject` `and as the common law:` `substantially the same as the common law` MEANS
    CONSIDER code
    WHEN `the Government Code` THEN `substantially the same as an existing statutory provision on the same subject`
    WHEN `the Civil Code` THEN `substantially the same as an existing statutory provision on the same subject` OR `substantially the same as the common law`
    WHEN `the Code of Civil Procedure` THEN `substantially the same as an existing statutory provision on the same subject`

-- Gov. Code § 9604: a reference by "any statute, charter or ordinance" to provisions carried into another statute as restatements and
-- continuations "shall, unless a contrary intent appears, be deemed a reference to the restatements and continuations."
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:15 | 9604. When the provisions of one statute are carried into another statute under circumstances in which they are required to be construed as restatements and continuations and not as new enactments, any reference made by any statute, charter or ordinance to such provisions shall, unless a contrary intent appears, be deemed a reference to the restatements and continuations.
@ref Gov. Code § 9604 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:15)
GIVEN `the provisions were carried into another statute and are required to be construed as restatements and continuations` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a contrary intent appears` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 9604 deems a reference to be a reference to the restatements and continuations` `the provisions were carried into another statute and are required to be construed as restatements and continuations` `a contrary intent appears` MEANS
    `the provisions were carried into another statute and are required to be construed as restatements and continuations` AND NOT `a contrary intent appears`

-- Gov. Code § 9: "Whenever reference is made to any portion of this code or of any other law of this State, the reference applies to all
-- amendments and additions now or hereafter made."
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:43 | 9. Whenever reference is made to any portion of this code or of any other law of this State, the reference applies to all amendments and additions now or hereafter made.
@ref Gov. Code § 9 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:43)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a reference to a portion of the Government Code or of another law of this State applies to its amendments and additions, whenever made` MEANS TRUE

-- Gov. Code § 5: "Unless the provision or the context otherwise requires, these general provisions, rules of construction, and
-- definitions shall govern the construction of this code."
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:16 | 5. Unless the provision or the context otherwise requires, these general provisions, rules of construction, and definitions shall govern the construction of this code.
@ref Gov. Code § 5 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:16)
GIVEN `the provision or the context otherwise requires` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the general provisions of the Government Code govern the construction of the code` `the provision or the context otherwise requires` MEANS
    NOT `the provision or the context otherwise requires`

§ `Retroactivity, pending proceedings and accrued rights`

-- Civ. Code § 3 and Code Civ. Proc. § 3: "No part of it is retroactive, unless expressly so declared."
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:16 | 3. No part of it is retroactive, unless expressly so declared.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:16 | 3. No part of it is retroactive, unless expressly so declared.
@ref Civ. Code § 3, Code Civ. Proc. § 3 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:16, src:CCP-ss-1-34:16)
GIVEN `the part expressly declares itself retroactive` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a part of the Civil Code or the Code of Civil Procedure is retroactive` `the part expressly declares itself retroactive` MEANS
    `the part expressly declares itself retroactive`

-- Gov. Code § 4, Civ. Code § 6, Code Civ. Proc. § 8: "No action or proceeding commenced before this code takes effect, and no right
-- accrued, is affected by" the code.  The three questions: is the proceeding or right protected from the code; must later procedure in
-- a pending proceeding conform to the code.
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:14 | 4. No action or proceeding commenced before this code takes effect, and no right accrued, is affected by this code, but all procedure thereafter taken therein shall conform to the provisions of this code so far as possible.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:22 | 6. No action or proceeding commenced before this Code takes effect, and no right accrued, is affected by its provisions.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:26 | 8. No action or proceeding commenced before this Code takes effect, and no right accrued, is affected by its provisions, but the proceedings therein must conform to the requirements of this Code as far as applicable.
@ref Gov. Code § 4, Civ. Code § 6, Code Civ. Proc. § 8 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:14, src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:22, src:CCP-ss-1-34:26)
GIVEN `the proceeding was commenced, or the right accrued, before the code took effect` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the section protects an action, proceeding or accrued right from the code` `the proceeding was commenced, or the right accrued, before the code took effect` MEANS
    `the proceeding was commenced, or the right accrued, before the code took effect`

-- Gov. Code § 4: "all procedure thereafter taken therein shall conform to the provisions of this code so far as possible"; Code Civ. Proc.
-- § 8: "the proceedings therein must conform to the requirements of this Code as far as applicable".  Civ. Code § 6 has no such sentence.
@ref Gov. Code § 4, Code Civ. Proc. § 8 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:14, src:CCP-ss-1-34:26)
GIVEN code IS A `Kind of code`
      `conformity is possible, or the requirement applicable` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`procedure taken after the code took effect in a proceeding begun before must conform to the code` code `conformity is possible, or the requirement applicable` MEANS
    CONSIDER code
    WHEN `the Government Code` THEN `conformity is possible, or the requirement applicable`
    WHEN `the Code of Civil Procedure` THEN `conformity is possible, or the requirement applicable`
    WHEN `the Civil Code` THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code § 6 says a proceeding begun before the code took effect is not affected by it, and has no sentence on whether later procedure conforms"

-- Code Civ. Proc. § 9: "the time that has already run shall be deemed part of the time prescribed as such limitation by this code",
-- when a limitation "has begun to run before this code goes into effect, and the same or any limitation is prescribed in this code".
-- The inputs are days; the days already run are counted from the day the period began to the day before the code took effect, and the text does
-- not say whether those days are counted with or without the day the code took effect (fork F-ccp9 in NOTES).
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:28 | 9. When a limitation or period of time prescribed in any existing statute for acquiring a right or barring a remedy, or for any other purpose, has begun to run before this code goes into effect, and the same or any limitation is prescribed in this code, the time that has already run shall be deemed part of the time prescribed as such limitation by this code.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 9 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:28)
GIVEN `the day the limitation began to run` IS A DATE
      `the day the code took effect` IS A DATE
      `the days the code prescribes` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`days left of a limitation that began to run before the code took effect` `the day the limitation began to run` `the day the code took effect` `the days the code prescribes` MEANS
    IF `the day the limitation began to run` LESS THAN `the day the code took effect`
    THEN `the days the code prescribes` MINUS (`the day the code took effect` MINUS `the day the limitation began to run`)
    ELSE REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 9 speaks of a limitation that has begun to run before the code goes into effect; this one did not"

§ `Offices and common law`

-- Gov. Code § 3: persons holding office under repealed acts, "which offices are continued by this code, continue to hold them according
-- to their former tenure."  Code Civ. Proc. § 6 says the same, "except those offices which are not continued by one of the Codes adopted at
-- this session"; § 7: an office abolished by repeal of an Act that "is not in substance reënacted or continued in either of the Codes ... ceases
-- at the time the Codes take effect."
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:12 | 3. All persons who at the time this code takes effect, hold office under any of the acts repealed by this code, which offices are continued by this code, continue to hold them according to their former tenure.
@ref Gov. Code § 3 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:12)
GIVEN `the office is continued by the Government Code` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a holder of an office under a repealed act continues to hold it under Gov. Code § 3` `the office is continued by the Government Code` MEANS
    IF `the office is continued by the Government Code`
    THEN TRUE
    ELSE REFUSE "Gov. Code § 3 speaks of offices that the code continues; it does not say what becomes of the holder of an office the code does not continue"

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:22 | 6. All persons who at the time this Code takes effect hold office under any of the Acts repealed, continue to hold the same according to the tenure thereof, except those offices which are not continued by one of the Codes adopted at this session of the Legislature.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:24 | 7. When any office is abolished by the repeal of any Act, and such Act is not in substance reënacted or continued in either of the Codes, such office ceases at the time the Codes take effect.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 6, § 7 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:22, src:CCP-ss-1-34:24)
GIVEN `the office is continued by one of the Codes adopted at the session` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a holder of an office under a repealed Act continues to hold it under Code Civ. Proc. § 6 and § 7` `the office is continued by one of the Codes adopted at the session` MEANS
    `the office is continued by one of the Codes adopted at the session`

-- Civ. Code § 22.2: "The common law of England, so far as it is not repugnant to or inconsistent with the Constitution of the United States,
-- or the Constitution or laws of this State, is the rule of decision in all the courts of this State."
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:88 | 22.2. The common law of England, so far as it is not repugnant to or inconsistent with the Constitution of the United States, or the Constitution or laws of this State, is the rule of decision in all the courts of this State.
@ref Civ. Code § 22.2 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:88)
GIVEN `the common law rule is repugnant to or inconsistent with the Constitution of the United States, or the Constitution or laws of this State` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the common law of England is the rule of decision` `the common law rule is repugnant to or inconsistent with the Constitution of the United States, or the Constitution or laws of this State` MEANS
    NOT `the common law rule is repugnant to or inconsistent with the Constitution of the United States, or the Constitution or laws of this State`

§ `Repeal by the codes`

-- Civ. Code § 20 and Code Civ. Proc. § 18: "No statute, law, or rule is continued in force because it is consistent with the provisions of
-- this Code on the same subject; but in all cases provided for by this Code, all statutes, laws, and rules heretofore in force ..., whether
-- consistent or not ..., unless expressly continued in force by it, are repealed or abrogated."  Consistency is not an input because the text
-- makes it irrelevant.  Where the code does not provide for the matter, the text does not say.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:77 | 20. No statute, law, or rule is continued in force because it is consistent with the provisions of this Code on the same subject; but in all cases provided for by this Code, all statutes, laws, and rules heretofore in force in this State, whether consistent or not with the provisions of this Code, unless expressly continued in force by it, are repealed or abrogated.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:78 | This repeal or abrogation does not revive any former law heretofore repealed, nor does it affect any right already existing or accrued, or any action or proceeding already taken, except as in this Code provided.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:84 | 18. No statute, law, or rule is continued in force because it is consistent with the provisions of this Code on the same subject; but in all cases provided for by this Code, all statutes, laws, and rules heretofore in force in this State, whether consistent or not with the provisions of this Code, unless expressly continued in force by it, are repealed and abrogated. This repeal or abrogation does not revive any former law heretofore repealed, nor does it affect any right already existing or accrued, or any action or proceeding already taken, except as in this Code provided; nor does it affect any private statute not expressly repealed.
@ref Civ. Code § 20, Code Civ. Proc. § 18 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:77-78, src:CCP-ss-1-34:84)
GIVEN `the code provides for the matter` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the code expressly continues the earlier law in force` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an earlier statute, law or rule remains in force` `the code provides for the matter` `the code expressly continues the earlier law in force` MEANS
    IF `the code provides for the matter`
    THEN `the code expressly continues the earlier law in force`
    ELSE REFUSE "Civ. Code § 20 and Code Civ. Proc. § 18 repeal earlier law in all cases provided for by the Code; they do not say what becomes of earlier law on a matter the Code does not provide for"

-- The same sections: "This repeal or abrogation does not revive any former law heretofore repealed, nor does it affect any right already
-- existing or accrued, or any action or proceeding already taken, except as in this Code provided".
@ref Civ. Code § 20, Code Civ. Proc. § 18 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:77-78, src:CCP-ss-1-34:84)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the repeal by the code revives a former law that was repealed before` MEANS FALSE

-- Code Civ. Proc. § 18 only: the repeal does not "affect any private statute not expressly repealed".
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 18 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:84)
GIVEN `the private statute is expressly repealed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the repeal by the Code of Civil Procedure affects a private statute` `the private statute is expressly repealed` MEANS
    `the private statute is expressly repealed`

§ `Conflicts among the provisions of the four 1872 codes`

-- Civ. Code § 23: the four codes "shall be construed as though each had been passed on the first day of the session".  The date of that
-- day is not in the deposited text.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:90 | 23. With relation to the laws passed at the 1872 Session of the Legislature, the Political Code, Civil Code, Code of Civil Procedure, and Penal Code, shall be construed as though each had been passed on the first day of the session.
@ref Civ. Code § 23 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:90)
GIVETH A DATE
`the day each of the four 1872 codes is construed as having been passed` MEANS
    REFUSE "Civ. Code § 23 says each of the four codes is construed as passed on the first day of the 1872 session, and no deposited section gives the date of that day"

-- Civ. Code § 23.1: provisions of any law passed at the 1872 session "which contravene or are inconsistent with the provisions of any of the four
-- codes ... prevail."
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:92 | 23.1. The provisions of any law passed at the 1872 Session of the Legislature which contravene or are inconsistent with the provisions of any of the four codes passed at the 1872 Session prevail.
@ref Civ. Code § 23.1 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:92)
GIVEN `the provision of the 1872-session law contravenes or is inconsistent with a provision of one of the four codes` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Civ. Code § 23.1 makes the provision of a law passed at the 1872 session prevail over the code provision` `the provision of the 1872-session law contravenes or is inconsistent with a provision of one of the four codes` MEANS
    `the provision of the 1872-session law contravenes or is inconsistent with a provision of one of the four codes`

-- Civ. Code § 23.2: as between themselves the four codes are construed "as though all such codes had been passed at the same moment of time and
-- were parts of the same statute", so none prevails over another by being later.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:94 | 23.2. With relation to each other, the provisions of the four codes shall be construed as though all such codes had been passed at the same moment of time and were parts of the same statute.
@ref Civ. Code § 23.2 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:94)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`one of the four 1872 codes prevails over another by having been passed later` MEANS FALSE

DECLARE `Unit in conflict` IS ONE OF
    `titles`
    `chapters of the same title`
    `articles of the same chapter`

DECLARE `Which provision the matter arises out of` IS ONE OF
    `the subject matter of the first provision's unit`
    `the subject matter of the second provision's unit`
    `the subject matter of neither`
    `the subject matter of both`

-- Civ. Code §§ 23.3-23.5: where titles, chapters of one title, or articles of one chapter conflict, "the provisions of each ... shall prevail as to all
-- matters and questions arising out of the subject matter of the" title, chapter or article.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:96 | 23.3. If the provisions of any title conflict with or contravene the provisions of another title, the provisions of each title shall prevail as to all matters and questions arising out of the subject matter of the title.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:98 | 23.4. If the provisions of any chapter conflict with or contravene the provisions of another chapter of the same title, the provisions of each chapter shall prevail as to all matters and questions arising out of the subject matter of the chapter.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:100 | 23.5. If the provisions of any article conflict with or contravene the provisions of another article of the same chapter, the provisions of each article shall prevail as to all matters and questions arising out of the subject matter of the article.
@ref Civ. Code § 23.3, § 23.4, § 23.5 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:96, src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:98, src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:100)
GIVEN unit IS A `Unit in conflict`
      matter IS A `Which provision the matter arises out of`
GIVETH A `Which of two statutes`
`the provision that prevails when units conflict` unit matter MEANS
    CONSIDER matter
    WHEN `the subject matter of the first provision's unit` THEN `the first statute`
    WHEN `the subject matter of the second provision's unit` THEN `the second statute`
    WHEN `the subject matter of neither` THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code §§ 23.3 to 23.5 make each provision prevail on matters arising out of the subject matter of its own title, chapter or article; they do not say which prevails on a matter arising out of neither"
    WHEN `the subject matter of both` THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code §§ 23.3 to 23.5 make each provision prevail on matters arising out of the subject matter of its own title, chapter or article; they do not say which prevails on a matter arising out of both"

-- Civ. Code § 23.6: for conflicting sections of one chapter or article, "the provisions of the sections last in numerical order shall prevail,
-- unless such construction is inconsistent with the meaning of the chapter or article."  The section numbers are given as numbers (17.5 for
-- section 17.5); sections numbered with a letter have no numerical place in the text (fork F-23.6 in NOTES).
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:102 | 23.6. If conflicting provisions are found in different sections of the same chapter or article, the provisions of the sections last in numerical order shall prevail, unless such construction is inconsistent with the meaning of the chapter or article.
@ref Civ. Code § 23.6 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:102)
GIVEN `the number of the first section` IS A NUMBER
      `the number of the second section` IS A NUMBER
      `that construction is inconsistent with the meaning of the chapter or article` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Which of two statutes`
`the section that prevails, the first being` `the number of the first section` `and the second` `the number of the second section` `and given that` `that construction is inconsistent with the meaning of the chapter or article` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `that construction is inconsistent with the meaning of the chapter or article`
            THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code § 23.6 makes the section last in numerical order prevail unless that is inconsistent with the meaning of the chapter or article, and does not say which section prevails then"
        IF `the number of the first section` EQUALS `the number of the second section`
            THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code § 23.6 turns on numerical order, and two sections with one number have none"
        IF `the number of the first section` GREATER THAN `the number of the second section` THEN `the first statute`
        OTHERWISE `the second statute`
